flow looped
note """
The SECOND end-to-end fixture. It exists because the first one drives no loop and
no send at run time, and step 5.2's construct list names both. Everything here is
lowered by the generator, and the sink prints a locked token from its own func
body so the assertion is on the PROGRAM's stdout rather than on the harness.

THE LOOP TERMINATES, which is the whole reason this is a separate fixture rather
than a clause added to the first. A loop is only runnable if something ends it:
bump raises the datum's own Attempt counter on every pass, ready routes on that
counter, and only the retrying outlet re-enters. The datum therefore makes
exactly attemptsBeforeDone passes and then leaves through finished. A fixture
whose loop did not terminate would hang rather than fail, which is the worst
shape a test can have.

THE SEND IS THE RE-ENTRY. `send hold -> again` merges hold's output back into the
flow preceding the node the label feeds, so the datum re-enters at bump. That is
the construct under test: it is what makes this a running proof of Flow.Send
rather than of text the emitter produced.

IT CARRIES NO checkpoint OR idempotent, for the same reason the first fixture
does not: a checkpoint would pull a journal, and with it a raft dependency, into
a test whose subject is the builder path.
"""

source ingest Feed()
loop again
transform bump Bump from ingest, again
branch ready Done from bump -> finished, retrying
transform hold Hold from retrying
send hold -> again
sink report Report from finished
